N2  - Summary This chapter explores the main technological, economic and environmental trends that will shape the industry of the future, and the challenges in reconciling innovation and responsibility. Smart industries are positioned at the heart of the technological, ecological and social upheavals that are shaping the 21st century. By 2050, they will be confronted with significant challenges: resource scarcity, climate instability, an aging workforce and an accelerated innovation cycle. The emergence of Biological Internet of Things (Bio-IoT) embodies an unprecedented convergence between biotechnology and information technology, heralding a new era where living beings interact symbiotically with machines. The quantum IoT represents a significant technological leap in the field of industrial communications. Digital twins are dynamic virtual replicas of physical objects, processes or systems, updated in real time through data streams from connected sensors.
